1
00:00:42,793 --> 00:00:43,669
Chris.

2
00:00:49,508 --> 00:00:50,843
Can we talk for a moment?

3
00:01:10,529 --> 00:01:11,405
Chris.

4
00:01:12,907 --> 00:01:14,533
She's the chairwoman of Pyeongyeon Group.

5
00:01:15,409 --> 00:01:17,953
I had no idea she'd visit out of the blue.

6
00:01:20,581 --> 00:01:22,708
They came without making a reservation.

7
00:01:23,501 --> 00:01:24,585
I'll send them away.

8
00:01:25,544 --> 00:01:28,672
We closed the kitchen for the night,
and we're out of ingredients too.

9
00:01:33,677 --> 00:01:34,512
No.

10
00:01:38,015 --> 00:01:39,558
Serve them, starting with wine.

11
00:01:40,142 --> 00:01:40,976
What?

12
00:01:41,060 --> 00:01:42,937
We're out of chicken.

13
00:01:43,771 --> 00:01:44,772
We'll serve fish instead.

14
00:01:44,855 --> 00:01:47,024
-Chris.
-I need to make the sauce from scratch.

15
00:01:47,107 --> 00:01:48,192
Chris.

16
00:01:50,986 --> 00:01:52,780
They're guests who came to try our food.

17
00:01:54,949 --> 00:01:56,408
I'll start on the appetizer.

18
00:02:24,687 --> 00:02:26,564
You messed up the fridge setting?

19
00:02:27,731 --> 00:02:28,566
And what?

20
00:02:28,649 --> 00:02:31,235
FRANCE, 8 YEARS AGO

21
00:02:31,861 --> 00:02:33,028
It all went bad?

22
00:02:33,946 --> 00:02:35,197
All of it?

23
00:02:40,703 --> 00:02:42,872
The fridge was set at 10°C, not 3°C?

24
00:02:44,498 --> 00:02:45,457
Do you know…

25
00:02:46,542 --> 00:02:48,002
what it took to get that ingredient?

26
00:02:50,212 --> 00:02:52,089
Do you know how much work it took?

27
00:02:53,966 --> 00:02:54,842
Do you know…

28
00:02:56,886 --> 00:02:58,178
what you've done?

29
00:03:00,389 --> 00:03:01,390
Get it.

30
00:03:02,433 --> 00:03:03,851
Get it, at whatever cost.

31
00:03:08,772 --> 00:03:10,274
Do whatever it takes to get it.

32
00:03:10,357 --> 00:03:11,609
Or else…

33
00:03:14,320 --> 00:03:15,613
I won't let you get away with this.

34
00:03:15,696 --> 00:03:17,114
Yes, Chef.

35
00:03:18,073 --> 00:03:19,199
I'll go and get it.

36
00:03:19,283 --> 00:03:20,576
I'll get it somehow.

37
00:03:20,659 --> 00:03:22,620
I'm sorry. I'm really sorry.

38
00:03:41,472 --> 00:03:42,348
Chef.

39
00:03:48,395 --> 00:03:52,524
The police say it was a speeding accident.

40
00:03:52,608 --> 00:03:58,155
The area is known for frequent accidents,
and it was also nighttime and foggy.

41
00:05:16,275 --> 00:05:17,401
CHEF CHA

42
00:05:19,278 --> 00:05:20,446
You can take it.

43
00:05:24,033 --> 00:05:24,867
Hey, Woo-jin.

44
00:05:25,743 --> 00:05:26,702
Are you busy?

45
00:05:27,619 --> 00:05:28,454
What?

46
00:05:33,584 --> 00:05:34,960
Will you come over if you're done?

47
00:05:36,712 --> 00:05:38,005
I'm at the bar drinking.

48
00:05:38,839 --> 00:05:40,049
By yourself?

49
00:05:40,132 --> 00:05:41,175
Yeah.

50
00:05:42,468 --> 00:05:44,636
It's fine. Go ahead.

51
00:05:54,229 --> 00:05:56,523
Sorry, I don't think I can make it.

52
00:05:57,274 --> 00:05:59,485
I'm out for dinner with Chairwoman Moon.

53
00:06:01,236 --> 00:06:02,321
Dinner?

54
00:06:03,572 --> 00:06:04,948
This late at night?

55
00:06:07,743 --> 00:06:08,786
Where?

56
00:06:11,705 --> 00:06:13,457
Well…

57
00:06:16,543 --> 00:06:17,461
We're at Famiglia.

58
00:06:18,045 --> 00:06:19,505
She wanted to visit.

59
00:06:26,678 --> 00:06:27,554
All right.

60
00:06:56,625 --> 00:06:59,253
I was worried
about the vague theme for the mission,

61
00:06:59,837 --> 00:07:04,049
but it was quite interesting to hear
how they incorporated it into the dishes.

62
00:07:04,633 --> 00:07:05,467
Right.

63
00:07:06,718 --> 00:07:10,180
It won't be easy
to keep coming up with such ideas.

64
00:07:10,264 --> 00:07:11,473
How will you manage?

65
00:07:11,557 --> 00:07:12,641
What about the security risk?

66
00:07:13,976 --> 00:07:16,979
We prepared many more themes
than we actually need,

67
00:07:17,062 --> 00:07:19,106
and we pick the actual mission
for each round

68
00:07:19,189 --> 00:07:20,858
at a secret location
two days before filming.

69
00:07:22,151 --> 00:07:25,445
Only the three judges and I
know what the mission is, so don't worry.

70
00:07:25,529 --> 00:07:27,906
Good. I expect you to keep that rule.

71
00:07:28,490 --> 00:07:31,160
Be extra careful
to keep Ms. Baek in the dark.

72
00:07:31,743 --> 00:07:33,704
You can't tell me anything either.

73
00:07:34,705 --> 00:07:35,747
I'll keep that in mind.

74
00:07:37,791 --> 00:07:38,792
So tell me.

75
00:07:39,543 --> 00:07:41,295
What do you expect
the first episode's ratings to be?

76
00:07:45,132 --> 00:07:50,179
I know you care more about the ratings
of the last episode than the first.

77
00:07:50,971 --> 00:07:52,890
Let's see whether this program

78
00:07:52,973 --> 00:07:56,101
will provide temporary relief
or a complete turnaround.

79
00:08:17,372 --> 00:08:20,792
I can't believe he's actually cooking
for people who showed up after closing,

80
00:08:20,876 --> 00:08:23,921
let alone
going to all this trouble for them.

81
00:08:24,004 --> 00:08:28,217
Those people won't stop at anything
to bring Famiglia down.

82
00:08:33,263 --> 00:08:36,516
I'd add poisonous mushrooms if I were him.

83
00:08:37,476 --> 00:08:39,144
Or taint the food with salmonella.

84
00:08:41,271 --> 00:08:43,649
Thinking about what happened
to my late brother makes my blood boil.

85
00:08:43,732 --> 00:08:45,150
Come on, Chef.

86
00:08:46,443 --> 00:08:47,402
Listen.

87
00:08:48,737 --> 00:08:51,657
I can't be as cool and open-minded as you.

88
00:09:15,722 --> 00:09:16,890
What is this?

89
00:09:17,516 --> 00:09:21,770
It's definitely tomato-based,
but it's so smooth.

90
00:09:26,358 --> 00:09:28,652
It has an almost nutty flavor.

91
00:09:29,861 --> 00:09:31,363
It's not just light.

92
00:09:32,322 --> 00:09:33,156
It's also rich.

93
00:09:52,384 --> 00:09:56,346
This dish isn't actually on our menu.

94
00:09:57,514 --> 00:09:59,766
I think the fish is a John Dory.

95
00:10:01,184 --> 00:10:03,061
How on earth did you make the sauce?

96
00:10:03,145 --> 00:10:06,273
The sauce is made with
thinly sliced dried pollock fillet,

97
00:10:06,356 --> 00:10:10,068
tomato, glutinous rice gochujang,
homemade makjang,

98
00:10:10,152 --> 00:10:12,070
and a little bit of heavy cream.

99
00:10:13,947 --> 00:10:19,244
The fish was also pan-fried,
then smoked with wood from an apple tree.

100
00:10:20,037 --> 00:10:22,831
I recommend you put
some fish on the spoon first,

101
00:10:22,914 --> 00:10:26,209
then top it
with the tomato gochujang sauce.

102
00:10:27,002 --> 00:10:27,836
Please enjoy.

103
00:11:27,729 --> 00:11:29,689
-Park.
-Yes, ma'am.

104
00:11:31,441 --> 00:11:33,443
Can you turn off the music?

105
00:11:35,028 --> 00:11:36,947
I'd like to enjoy the ride
in silence today.

106
00:11:37,823 --> 00:11:39,449
Yes, of course.

107
00:11:44,788 --> 00:11:47,040
Why do you only take sous-chef roles?

108
00:11:49,042 --> 00:11:52,671
You have the skill
to be a better head chef than most.

109
00:11:53,338 --> 00:11:54,673
Wouldn't it be because it's easier?

110
00:11:54,756 --> 00:11:56,591
I don't have to worry
about being responsible.

111
00:11:56,675 --> 00:11:59,719
The sous-chef doesn't have to worry
about the restaurant or sales.

112
00:12:02,013 --> 00:12:03,849
What a boring answer.

113
00:12:06,643 --> 00:12:08,145
I want to stay flexible.

114
00:12:09,729 --> 00:12:11,398
It makes it easier to travel

115
00:12:11,481 --> 00:12:13,775
and learn many things in different places.

116
00:12:24,995 --> 00:12:27,122
Some people pretend to praise you

117
00:12:27,205 --> 00:12:29,207
while still discriminating against Asians,

118
00:12:30,041 --> 00:12:32,377
and I don't want to grovel to them
for a head chef title.

119
00:12:34,713 --> 00:12:36,673
I didn't think I was ready

120
00:12:37,382 --> 00:12:39,759
to open a restaurant under my own name.

121
00:12:42,929 --> 00:12:44,014
I thought

122
00:12:44,806 --> 00:12:46,224
I still had some learning to do.

123
00:12:49,227 --> 00:12:50,729
There can be many explanatory reasons.

124
00:12:51,938 --> 00:12:54,191
We just don't know which of them are true.

125
00:12:58,528 --> 00:13:00,405
Did that answer your question?

126
00:13:04,451 --> 00:13:06,077
Why did you come back to Korea?

127
00:13:07,746 --> 00:13:09,289
Are you looking for your birth parents?

128
00:13:12,042 --> 00:13:13,293
When did you move to the US?

129
00:13:13,376 --> 00:13:15,670
Before you answer that,
what early memories do you have?

130
00:13:16,630 --> 00:13:18,798
You signed to work
at a restaurant in New York.

131
00:13:18,882 --> 00:13:20,926
Why did you stay to enter this competition

132
00:13:21,009 --> 00:13:23,386
knowing you'd have to pay the penalty
for breach of contract?

133
00:13:23,470 --> 00:13:25,639
And why are you representing Famiglia?

134
00:13:29,601 --> 00:13:30,852
I'm sorry.

135
00:13:30,936 --> 00:13:32,312
I asked too many questions, didn't I?

136
00:13:34,231 --> 00:13:35,065
Just one.

137
00:13:36,233 --> 00:13:39,194
Ask just one question,
then let's call it a day.

138
00:13:40,570 --> 00:13:41,905
I'm really tired.

139
00:13:46,576 --> 00:13:47,410
Okay.

140
00:13:48,036 --> 00:13:49,746
Then just one.

141
00:13:55,627 --> 00:13:57,170
Is there someone you love?

142
00:14:00,840 --> 00:14:01,841
Do you…

143
00:14:03,260 --> 00:14:04,594
love someone?

144
00:14:18,733 --> 00:14:19,568
My dad.

145
00:14:19,651 --> 00:14:20,694
What?

146
00:14:22,612 --> 00:14:23,572
My father.

147
00:14:26,324 --> 00:14:27,701
The person I love the most.

148
00:14:28,827 --> 00:14:29,995
The person I respect the most.

149
00:14:31,746 --> 00:14:33,039
The person I miss the most.

150
00:14:34,749 --> 00:14:35,584
My dad.

151
00:14:39,004 --> 00:14:41,381
The American father who adopted you?

152
00:14:42,424 --> 00:14:43,508
My birth father.

153
00:14:45,468 --> 00:14:47,220
He taught me how to cook.

154
00:14:48,597 --> 00:14:49,848
He made me what I am now.

155
00:14:50,599 --> 00:14:53,476
Was your birth father a chef too?

156
00:14:54,352 --> 00:14:56,229
Does he still cook--

157
00:14:56,313 --> 00:14:58,106
I don't dare compare myself to him.

158
00:14:58,690 --> 00:15:00,358
He's that great a chef.

159
00:15:01,151 --> 00:15:01,985
No.

160
00:15:02,485 --> 00:15:03,820
You could even say

161
00:15:05,071 --> 00:15:06,281
he's the best chef in the world.

162
00:15:08,533 --> 00:15:10,035
I grew up eating his food

163
00:15:11,077 --> 00:15:12,954
and learning how to cook from him.

164
00:15:13,872 --> 00:15:17,208
We crossed fields and climbed mountains
to taste many things.

165
00:15:18,335 --> 00:15:20,170
My dad always told me

166
00:15:21,421 --> 00:15:22,589
that cooking

167
00:15:23,465 --> 00:15:26,468
wasn't just about making something
that tasted good.

168
00:15:29,554 --> 00:15:33,183
My dad doesn't make what tastes good.

169
00:15:35,518 --> 00:15:39,147
He makes food that touches the heart.

170
00:15:44,235 --> 00:15:45,236
My dream

171
00:15:46,780 --> 00:15:48,948
is to become a chef he'd be proud of.

172
00:15:50,992 --> 00:15:52,369
If only I can, that is.

173
00:15:53,036 --> 00:15:54,329
If only I could…

174
00:15:56,581 --> 00:15:59,209
make something
that my dad would approve of.

175
00:16:06,883 --> 00:16:07,884
Where is he now?

176
00:16:11,179 --> 00:16:12,180
Your father.

177
00:16:18,603 --> 00:16:19,813
He's here with me.

178
00:16:30,156 --> 00:16:31,533
Even at this moment,

179
00:16:32,242 --> 00:16:33,743
he's here somewhere.

180
00:17:10,196 --> 00:17:11,072
What's up with you?

181
00:17:11,156 --> 00:17:12,741
You said you went drinking last night.

182
00:17:12,824 --> 00:17:14,701
You're playing like
you ate a handful of wild ginseng.

183
00:17:49,652 --> 00:17:50,862
What got into you yesterday?

184
00:17:50,945 --> 00:17:52,363
You rarely drink.

185
00:17:53,656 --> 00:17:55,116
Now we're eating hangover soup together.

186
00:18:02,081 --> 00:18:03,082
Don't tell me

187
00:18:03,792 --> 00:18:05,251
it's because you lost to Chris K. again.

188
00:18:08,421 --> 00:18:09,255
Hold on.

189
00:18:15,637 --> 00:18:16,513
You knew?

190
00:18:18,932 --> 00:18:19,933
Is that why

191
00:18:21,184 --> 00:18:22,393
you got him involved?

192
00:18:24,312 --> 00:18:25,396
To get me to participate?

193
00:18:25,480 --> 00:18:26,689
Don't get the wrong idea.

194
00:18:27,565 --> 00:18:30,068
I still believe
that you'll win the competition.

195
00:18:30,985 --> 00:18:32,779
I don't care
how well the first episode does.

196
00:18:33,488 --> 00:18:34,572
As always,

197
00:18:35,865 --> 00:18:37,951
it's the ratings
of the last episode that count.

198
00:18:42,705 --> 00:18:43,706
I believe

199
00:18:44,624 --> 00:18:46,918
you'll give us the grand finale we need.

200
00:18:50,839 --> 00:18:51,673
Listen to me carefully.

201
00:18:56,261 --> 00:18:57,387
I'm Cha Woo-jin.

202
00:18:59,389 --> 00:19:02,016
I'll never hand over the trophy to him.

203
00:19:34,507 --> 00:19:37,719
Slowly. That's it.

204
00:19:38,219 --> 00:19:39,053
All right.

205
00:19:40,179 --> 00:19:42,015
Drink some water first.

206
00:19:42,891 --> 00:19:45,268
It's warm water, so drink it slowly.

207
00:19:48,897 --> 00:19:50,315
Your medicine.

208
00:19:55,653 --> 00:19:57,280
There you go. Good girl.

209
00:19:58,281 --> 00:19:59,115
Here.

210
00:20:07,123 --> 00:20:08,416
Did you swallow it?

211
00:20:10,084 --> 00:20:11,252
Let's lie back down.

212
00:20:15,465 --> 00:20:18,426
Your caregiver will be here soon.

213
00:20:19,010 --> 00:20:20,011
Let's wait for her.

214
00:20:20,094 --> 00:20:21,137
Okay.

215
00:20:22,138 --> 00:20:23,222
Dad.

216
00:20:24,265 --> 00:20:26,601
Don't worry about me.

217
00:20:26,684 --> 00:20:29,520
As always, work hard

218
00:20:29,604 --> 00:20:31,648
and make good food.

219
00:20:35,610 --> 00:20:36,527
When was the last time

220
00:20:37,195 --> 00:20:40,907
this child smiled brightly?

221
00:20:41,741 --> 00:20:44,160
I have a daughter.

222
00:20:44,786 --> 00:20:46,037
PARK JE-HYEON
OWNER-CHEF OF BINGSAK

223
00:20:46,120 --> 00:20:49,374
She's more precious to me
than anything else in the world.

224
00:20:49,999 --> 00:20:51,250
My daughter…

225
00:20:54,462 --> 00:20:56,631
suffers from a heart condition.

226
00:20:57,548 --> 00:21:00,093
My wife passed away
from the same condition.

227
00:21:08,434 --> 00:21:09,477
BINGSAK
LUNCH

228
00:21:09,560 --> 00:21:11,854
BINGSAK

229
00:21:11,938 --> 00:21:14,399
Everything I've done,
from moving to Jeju Island

230
00:21:14,482 --> 00:21:17,193
to changing my cooking style
to healthy Mediterranean-inspired dishes

231
00:21:17,276 --> 00:21:19,737
that bring out the natural flavors
of the ingredients,

232
00:21:19,821 --> 00:21:21,906
has been for my daughter.

233
00:21:23,783 --> 00:21:25,368
Nothing makes me happier

234
00:21:25,451 --> 00:21:29,580
than seeing my daughter enjoy my food
and eat well.

235
00:21:32,083 --> 00:21:33,918
I named my restaurant "Bingsak"

236
00:21:34,752 --> 00:21:38,798
because I want to see my daughter smile,
even if it's just once more.

237
00:21:40,591 --> 00:21:44,053
In Jeju dialect,
"bingsak" means "a bright smile."

238
00:21:50,101 --> 00:21:51,310
I told you to use the millstone.

239
00:21:52,186 --> 00:21:53,438
Who told you to use a blender?

240
00:21:54,147 --> 00:21:55,440
Didn't I tell you many times

241
00:21:55,523 --> 00:21:57,775
that the motor's heat
turns the ingredients bitter?

242
00:21:57,859 --> 00:21:58,693
JANG OK-SEON
OWNER-CHEF OF ONGOJISIM

243
00:21:58,776 --> 00:21:59,986
Did you forget, or was this deliberate?

244
00:22:03,865 --> 00:22:06,242
You resorted to tricks
just to save a few minutes?

245
00:22:06,325 --> 00:22:09,287
Tell me if you don't want to do this.
I won't force a task on you.

246
00:22:10,413 --> 00:22:11,456
No, Chef.

247
00:22:11,539 --> 00:22:12,832
I apologize.

248
00:22:13,875 --> 00:22:14,876
How many times did I tell you?

249
00:22:16,169 --> 00:22:17,253
Tradition

250
00:22:18,504 --> 00:22:19,589
is devotion.

251
00:22:21,174 --> 00:22:22,300
Yes, Chef.

252
00:22:31,350 --> 00:22:32,393
Here's five million won.

253
00:22:34,562 --> 00:22:35,980
I see five million,

254
00:22:36,064 --> 00:22:37,482
and raise you another five million.

255
00:22:43,488 --> 00:22:44,322
Call.

256
00:22:45,823 --> 00:22:47,158
Full house.

257
00:22:50,203 --> 00:22:51,913
Man, I finally won.

258
00:22:52,789 --> 00:22:53,706
Four nines.

259
00:22:58,252 --> 00:23:01,005
Thank you very much for all this.

260
00:23:01,089 --> 00:23:04,717
What kind of a game
can't be won with a full house?

261
00:23:05,551 --> 00:23:06,886
Seriously.

262
00:23:07,804 --> 00:23:09,388
Damn it!

263
00:23:16,229 --> 00:23:20,024
YANG JA-RYONG
HEAD CHEF OF GOLDEN DRAGON

264
00:23:20,900 --> 00:23:24,904
You know how much
you borrowed from us so far, right?

265
00:23:26,322 --> 00:23:28,116
Including today's loan,

266
00:23:28,199 --> 00:23:30,493
you owe us a total of 378 million won.

267
00:23:31,577 --> 00:23:32,620
I know.

268
00:23:33,412 --> 00:23:36,499
I just need more time.

269
00:23:39,710 --> 00:23:41,629
I gave you my word.

270
00:23:41,712 --> 00:23:44,132
I'll pay you back immediately
with the prize money

271
00:23:44,215 --> 00:23:45,800
I win from the competition.

272
00:23:45,883 --> 00:23:46,843
Okay?

273
00:23:46,926 --> 00:23:48,302
I know you did.

274
00:23:48,928 --> 00:23:51,097
You should pay us back
the very day you win.

275
00:23:51,180 --> 00:23:52,306
If you win, that is.

276
00:23:52,890 --> 00:23:53,850
But tell me.

277
00:23:56,269 --> 00:23:57,478
Can you win?

278
00:23:58,729 --> 00:23:59,564
Can you?

279
00:24:00,314 --> 00:24:01,649
If you can't…

280
00:24:06,195 --> 00:24:08,072
we'll drain the juice

281
00:24:08,156 --> 00:24:10,366
out of this fat dumpling.

282
00:24:19,208 --> 00:24:20,710
Is the duxelles not done yet?

283
00:24:20,793 --> 00:24:21,627
It's done, Chef.

284
00:24:21,711 --> 00:24:22,587
Meringue.

285
00:24:22,670 --> 00:24:24,130
Yes, Chef. It's done.

286
00:24:25,006 --> 00:24:26,382
Michael, get the pastry bag.

287
00:24:26,465 --> 00:24:27,300
Yes, Chef.

288
00:24:34,098 --> 00:24:35,099
It needs more color.

289
00:24:35,183 --> 00:24:36,642
Make sure it doesn't burn at the sides.

290
00:24:37,268 --> 00:24:38,603
Chef Park, let's see the carrots.

291
00:24:40,563 --> 00:24:41,814
-Cook it some more.
-Yes, Chef.

292
00:24:44,525 --> 00:24:45,943
Okay. This will do.

293
00:24:46,027 --> 00:24:47,570
Put them in the oven. It'll take a while.

294
00:24:47,653 --> 00:24:48,487
Yes, Chef.

295
00:24:48,988 --> 00:24:51,240
Chef Park, don't squeeze out
too much moisture when you blend it.

296
00:24:51,324 --> 00:24:53,201
-Got it.
-Okay. You're doing great.

297
00:24:53,284 --> 00:24:54,660
But let's pick up the pace a bit.

298
00:24:54,744 --> 00:24:55,745
-Yes, Chef!
-Yes, Chef!

299
00:25:06,339 --> 00:25:09,592
The episode's just 30 minutes in,
and there are over 7,000 comments.

300
00:25:09,675 --> 00:25:11,093
I sense something big is coming.

301
00:25:11,177 --> 00:25:13,930
The chefs look even better on TV.

302
00:25:14,013 --> 00:25:15,806
There's something about men in uniform.

303
00:25:15,890 --> 00:25:17,183
It's only the first episode,

304
00:25:17,266 --> 00:25:19,977
and everyone's saying
Chef Cha Woo-jin will win.

305
00:25:23,272 --> 00:25:25,983
PYEONGYEON GROUP CHAIRWOMAN MOON HAE-SEON

306
00:25:26,067 --> 00:25:27,944
Good job. It looks great.

307
00:25:28,444 --> 00:25:31,322
The ratings will peak from the start.
I'm willing to bet on it.

308
00:25:32,031 --> 00:25:32,865
However…

309
00:25:35,284 --> 00:25:37,078
Famiglia is getting most of the attention.

310
00:25:37,161 --> 00:25:39,747
You didn't make them
look good on purpose, did you?

311
00:25:39,830 --> 00:25:40,831
I'm kidding.

312
00:25:43,668 --> 00:25:45,836
ROUND 2
SPLENDOR IN THE GRASS

313
00:25:48,881 --> 00:25:50,258
"Splendor in the Grass."

314
00:25:51,676 --> 00:25:54,345
That is the theme for the second round.

315
00:25:55,012 --> 00:25:57,223
Are you just as flustered
as in the first round?

316
00:25:58,099 --> 00:26:00,142
You still don't know what's going on?

317
00:26:00,226 --> 00:26:03,604
However, we believe in you.

318
00:26:04,730 --> 00:26:07,066
The chefs' creative
and artistic interpretations.

319
00:26:11,529 --> 00:26:13,406
Flashy yet moving dishes.

320
00:26:16,200 --> 00:26:18,744
I look forward to seeing
what you'll create.

321
00:26:24,500 --> 00:26:26,752
ROUND 1 COMPLETE

322
00:26:36,387 --> 00:26:39,849
ROUND 2 THEME
SPLENDOR IN THE GRASS

323
00:26:48,774 --> 00:26:49,650
All done.

324
00:26:50,276 --> 00:26:51,110
All right.

325
00:26:52,486 --> 00:26:53,487
Get ready.

326
00:26:54,196 --> 00:26:56,449
Bottoms up!

327
00:26:56,532 --> 00:26:58,659
-Cheers!
-Cheers!

328
00:27:07,626 --> 00:27:09,337
Come on, guys.

329
00:27:09,420 --> 00:27:11,297
It's a rule to empty the first glass.

330
00:27:12,506 --> 00:27:13,341
What's wrong with you men?

331
00:27:29,357 --> 00:27:32,651
What do you think
the theme for this round means?

332
00:27:33,277 --> 00:27:35,821
To be honest, the last one was difficult,

333
00:27:35,905 --> 00:27:37,698
but at least we got two lines from a poem.

334
00:27:39,408 --> 00:27:41,786
Do they expect an essay from us this time?

335
00:27:41,869 --> 00:27:43,996
I don't get
what kind of competition this is.

336
00:27:44,538 --> 00:27:45,956
Me neither.

337
00:27:46,040 --> 00:27:48,876
We might end up
taking the college entrance exam next.

338
00:27:49,585 --> 00:27:52,546
"Splendor in the Grass."

339
00:27:55,132 --> 00:27:57,009
Are we to ride horses in a plain?

340
00:27:58,260 --> 00:27:59,095
Wait.

341
00:27:59,678 --> 00:28:01,680
Do we have to cook with horse meat?

342
00:28:04,225 --> 00:28:05,059
No.

343
00:28:05,142 --> 00:28:07,061
What does "splendor" mean?

344
00:28:20,574 --> 00:28:23,452
Why worry? We have Chris.

345
00:28:26,080 --> 00:28:27,123
Shall we eat?

346
00:28:40,553 --> 00:28:41,554
It's tough, isn't it?

347
00:28:46,308 --> 00:28:47,309
It is for me too.

348
00:28:49,687 --> 00:28:50,855
It's tough.

349
00:28:54,608 --> 00:28:56,110
But we don't have a choice.

350
00:29:00,281 --> 00:29:01,574
Someone has to do it.

351
00:29:04,452 --> 00:29:05,536
Right?

352
00:29:09,290 --> 00:29:10,958
Famiglia isn't what's important.

353
00:29:22,261 --> 00:29:23,262
If not me…

354
00:29:27,475 --> 00:29:28,726
who can do it?

355
00:29:33,105 --> 00:29:34,190
If not us…

356
00:29:37,860 --> 00:29:39,153
who can do it?

357
00:29:55,878 --> 00:29:57,171
Great job today, Ms. Baek.

358
00:29:57,254 --> 00:29:58,255
Thank you.

359
00:29:58,339 --> 00:30:00,174
How did the meeting go?

360
00:30:00,257 --> 00:30:01,884
Good. It went pretty well.

361
00:30:02,593 --> 00:30:03,594
It worked out for me.

362
00:30:03,677 --> 00:30:06,514
The ratings peaked from the first episode.

363
00:30:07,223 --> 00:30:11,727
Koreans sure love everything
about food and eating, don't they?

364
00:30:12,561 --> 00:30:14,730
It was very entertaining.

365
00:30:14,813 --> 00:30:16,690
I mean,
all those flashy dishes and whatnot.

366
00:30:16,774 --> 00:30:17,608
I have to say,

367
00:30:17,691 --> 00:30:20,861
Jeong-won is pretty good at what she does.

368
00:30:20,945 --> 00:30:22,029
Thanks to her hard work,

369
00:30:22,112 --> 00:30:25,407
I saved face at the CEO meeting today.

370
00:30:26,659 --> 00:30:27,743
Anyway,

371
00:30:27,826 --> 00:30:32,456
what on earth does
"Splendor in the Grass" even mean?

372
00:30:34,542 --> 00:30:35,584
Mr. Jeon.

373
00:30:36,627 --> 00:30:37,503
What could it mean?

374
00:30:38,087 --> 00:30:39,505
-Well…
-Yes?

375
00:30:39,588 --> 00:30:41,590
I have no idea at all.

376
00:30:42,716 --> 00:30:44,093
The viewers want to know too.

377
00:30:44,176 --> 00:30:46,720
Everyone's making a guess.

378
00:30:47,596 --> 00:30:48,847
There's a massive response.

379
00:30:50,266 --> 00:30:53,811
I was worried
the public would find it too complicated.

380
00:30:53,894 --> 00:30:54,728
So that's a relief.

381
00:30:56,313 --> 00:30:57,231
But wait.

382
00:30:57,731 --> 00:31:01,026
I know security is important,
but shouldn't they tell me?

383
00:31:01,110 --> 00:31:03,195
I mean, I'm the CEO, after all.

384
00:31:03,279 --> 00:31:06,073
All the CEOs grilled me for hints,

385
00:31:06,156 --> 00:31:07,825
and all I could say was, "I don't know."

386
00:31:07,908 --> 00:31:09,952
It was humiliating!

387
00:31:10,035 --> 00:31:11,287
You're right.

388
00:31:11,829 --> 00:31:15,207
Shouldn't you at least know what it means?

389
00:31:15,291 --> 00:31:16,959
-That way--
-But then,

390
00:31:17,668 --> 00:31:20,588
I can't complain
because not even the chairwoman knows.

391
00:31:20,671 --> 00:31:21,505
Right.

392
00:31:23,007 --> 00:31:24,592
Two days before the episode was filmed,

393
00:31:24,675 --> 00:31:27,219
I took Ms. Seon and the three judges

394
00:31:27,303 --> 00:31:29,054
to that place you know that I go often.

395
00:31:29,138 --> 00:31:30,973
-Chowon Chambok--
-Yes, Chowon Chambokguk.

396
00:31:31,056 --> 00:31:36,020
I bought them a meal there,
and they all loved the pufferfish soup.

397
00:31:37,438 --> 00:31:39,023
"Chowon" means "grassland."
That must be it!

398
00:31:41,108 --> 00:31:41,942
Is it not?

399
00:31:42,443 --> 00:31:43,611
Oh, I don't know!

400
00:31:50,159 --> 00:31:51,327
What?

401
00:31:52,119 --> 00:31:53,245
Pufferfish?

402
00:31:58,626 --> 00:31:59,585
Yes, In-seop.

403
00:32:00,586 --> 00:32:03,130
They got the idea from Chowon Chambokguk.

404
00:32:10,179 --> 00:32:13,182
The day they chose
the theme for round two,

405
00:32:13,849 --> 00:32:16,727
Ms. Baek Hyeon-ju took Ms. Seon
and the judges to Chowon Chambokguk,

406
00:32:16,810 --> 00:32:18,729
and they all raved about the food.

407
00:32:19,897 --> 00:32:20,939
And that very afternoon

408
00:32:21,023 --> 00:32:23,734
is when they settled on grass
as the theme.

409
00:32:23,817 --> 00:32:26,820
She thinks it had
something to do with Chowon Chambokguk.

410
00:32:27,863 --> 00:32:28,906
That restaurant

411
00:32:29,490 --> 00:32:31,241
is Ms. Baek's favorite place.

412
00:32:31,325 --> 00:32:32,826
And it's famous too.

413
00:32:34,703 --> 00:32:35,871
-Chef Yoo.
-Yes?

414
00:32:38,874 --> 00:32:40,334
Don't overthink it.

415
00:32:41,168 --> 00:32:44,505
Experts often defy expectations
by doing something simple.

416
00:32:45,589 --> 00:32:47,174
You know who Baek Hyeon-ju is.

417
00:32:47,257 --> 00:32:48,926
She's the CEO of Pyeongyeon Media.

418
00:32:50,511 --> 00:32:52,012
She knows what she's saying.

419
00:32:54,890 --> 00:32:57,559
Pufferfish is the main ingredient.

420
00:33:01,313 --> 00:33:03,190
Prepare something with pufferfish.

421
00:33:05,359 --> 00:33:06,318
Pufferfish.

422
00:33:07,986 --> 00:33:09,279
No!

423
00:33:09,363 --> 00:33:11,073
No!

424
00:33:15,369 --> 00:33:19,873
I told you to get personal details,
not write a thesis.

425
00:33:20,624 --> 00:33:22,418
The scope, the world of his cooking.

426
00:33:22,501 --> 00:33:24,086
The philosophy surrounding his cooking.

427
00:33:24,169 --> 00:33:25,754
Which school he went to
and how well he did.

428
00:33:25,838 --> 00:33:27,047
How people in his field view him.

429
00:33:27,131 --> 00:33:28,799
Why he only works as a sous-chef.

430
00:33:28,882 --> 00:33:30,843
None of that is important to me!

431
00:33:30,926 --> 00:33:32,052
Damn it!

432
00:33:34,555 --> 00:33:35,514
I'm sorry, ma'am.

433
00:33:35,597 --> 00:33:36,640
I apologize.

434
00:33:47,025 --> 00:33:48,986
Which drama genre
is the most entertaining?

435
00:33:50,404 --> 00:33:52,072
Come on, answer the question.

436
00:33:52,781 --> 00:33:53,615
Action?

437
00:33:53,699 --> 00:33:56,618
No, a soap opera with twists and turns.

438
00:33:56,702 --> 00:33:58,287
Secrets surrounding one's birth,

439
00:33:58,370 --> 00:34:01,206
forbidden love,
revenge, cheating partners.

440
00:34:01,290 --> 00:34:03,208
Is this person an orphan
or someone's relative?

441
00:34:03,292 --> 00:34:06,336
Is he actually dating his own sibling?

442
00:34:06,420 --> 00:34:08,881
Is this someone who wants revenge
and came back in disguise

443
00:34:08,964 --> 00:34:10,758
or after getting plastic surgery?

444
00:34:10,841 --> 00:34:14,178
Or is someone blackmailing him
for what he did in the past?

445
00:34:14,261 --> 00:34:18,182
Who did they kill long ago?

446
00:34:18,265 --> 00:34:19,224
Ms. Baek…

447
00:34:19,808 --> 00:34:22,269
That's what makes a really fun drama.

448
00:34:22,352 --> 00:34:24,021
A trashy soap opera!

449
00:34:39,495 --> 00:34:42,915
What I want to know is stuff like that.

450
00:34:43,624 --> 00:34:47,169
And that's what others want to know too.

451
00:34:47,252 --> 00:34:48,337
Right.

452
00:34:51,924 --> 00:34:53,884
Chris K.'s childhood?

453
00:34:55,219 --> 00:34:56,428
I have no idea.

454
00:34:56,512 --> 00:35:00,933
I don't know anything about his childhood,
and there aren't any records either.

455
00:35:01,767 --> 00:35:03,977
I got the impression that he doesn't like
talking about his past.

456
00:35:05,145 --> 00:35:06,063
I see.

457
00:35:07,523 --> 00:35:08,357
Is there no way

458
00:35:08,982 --> 00:35:11,902
to find out when he moved to the US?

459
00:35:12,861 --> 00:35:14,404
No. No one knows that either.

460
00:35:19,201 --> 00:35:21,912
He did mention his father though.

461
00:35:24,164 --> 00:35:25,040
His father?

462
00:35:26,500 --> 00:35:27,709
What did he say?

463
00:35:28,585 --> 00:35:30,003
Does he live with his father?

464
00:35:35,259 --> 00:35:36,343
Hello.

465
00:35:36,885 --> 00:35:37,761
You got here early.

466
00:35:37,845 --> 00:35:39,096
-Oh, hello.
-Hello.

467
00:35:39,179 --> 00:35:41,765
It took me an hour just to leave Seoul.

468
00:35:42,766 --> 00:35:44,017
Seoul's traffic is just

469
00:35:44,768 --> 00:35:45,811
so unpredictable.

470
00:35:45,894 --> 00:35:47,813
Thank you for making the journey.

471
00:35:47,896 --> 00:35:50,899
I know you're all busy,
so should we start the meeting right away?

472
00:36:23,432 --> 00:36:24,349
Song.

473
00:36:27,060 --> 00:36:27,895
Song.

474
00:36:54,755 --> 00:36:55,589
Chris?

475
00:37:01,803 --> 00:37:06,558
What precious ingredient are we off to get
that we must leave at the crack of dawn?

476
00:37:07,351 --> 00:37:10,729
I closed the restaurant,
cleaned the kitchen, cleaned the house,

477
00:37:10,812 --> 00:37:13,190
and went to bed at 3 a.m.,
but you woke me at 5 a.m.

478
00:37:13,273 --> 00:37:15,108
Don't you think that's too cruel?

479
00:37:16,401 --> 00:37:19,947
Not to mention
we film the second round tomorrow.

480
00:37:20,030 --> 00:37:22,616
Why do you want to
climb a mountain right now?

481
00:37:23,784 --> 00:37:26,954
Do we really need whatever it is
that we're off to get?

482
00:37:58,110 --> 00:37:59,945
I have a bad feeling about this.

483
00:38:01,363 --> 00:38:04,366
Do they illegally catch snakes
and make something with them?

484
00:38:04,950 --> 00:38:06,952
I hate snakes
more than anything in the world.

485
00:38:48,410 --> 00:38:49,619
Thank you, sir.

486
00:38:49,703 --> 00:38:51,705
-No problem. Go on then.
-Bye.

487
00:39:00,672 --> 00:39:01,631
What are you doing?

488
00:39:03,008 --> 00:39:06,178
I'm not used to places like this.

489
00:39:07,054 --> 00:39:08,096
We can go now.

490
00:39:10,182 --> 00:39:11,266
What do you have?

491
00:39:12,225 --> 00:39:13,060
Oh, this?

492
00:39:14,770 --> 00:39:16,188
It's nuruk,
a traditional fermentation starter.

493
00:39:17,564 --> 00:39:18,523
Nuruk?

494
00:39:21,443 --> 00:39:23,612
Oh my. They're so pretty.

495
00:39:26,490 --> 00:39:27,616
This is nuruk?

496
00:39:28,658 --> 00:39:31,661
I didn't know it was so pretty.

497
00:39:33,205 --> 00:39:35,999
What are you going to do with this?

498
00:39:39,836 --> 00:39:42,089
Let's get back first. We can think then.

499
00:39:48,011 --> 00:39:49,638
Song. Let's go.

500
00:39:52,891 --> 00:39:55,560
Are you ready to cook up something

501
00:39:55,644 --> 00:39:57,312
that will move us all?

502
00:39:57,395 --> 00:39:58,355
-Yes!
-Yes!

503
00:40:01,399 --> 00:40:04,152
As you were informed last week,
the theme of this round

504
00:40:04,236 --> 00:40:06,071
is "Splendor in the Grass."

505
00:40:06,154 --> 00:40:07,197
ROUND 2
SPLENDOR IN THE GRASS

506
00:40:07,280 --> 00:40:13,078
What dishes will the greatest chefs
who have gathered here today make for us?

507
00:40:16,164 --> 00:40:18,500
Thump, thump, thump, thump.

508
00:40:19,668 --> 00:40:21,628
My heart beats with anticipation.

509
00:40:24,256 --> 00:40:27,551
I know our viewers are eager to find out,

510
00:40:28,135 --> 00:40:31,138
so here is the mission.

511
00:40:33,056 --> 00:40:35,433
MARBLING

512
00:40:36,017 --> 00:40:40,188
What do you check first when you buy beef?

513
00:40:41,273 --> 00:40:43,191
Whether it's locally produced or imported?

514
00:40:43,275 --> 00:40:45,443
Or whether it's from the US or Australia?

515
00:40:45,527 --> 00:40:48,029
Many of you check the country of origin,

516
00:40:49,156 --> 00:40:51,116
but don't you also check the grading?

517
00:40:51,199 --> 00:40:53,368
Prime grade or choice grade.

518
00:40:53,451 --> 00:40:55,078
In other words, the marbling grade.

519
00:40:58,915 --> 00:41:03,336
Marbling is the amount of fat
distributed within the lean meat.

520
00:41:03,420 --> 00:41:07,340
The higher the fat content,
the higher the marbling score.

521
00:41:10,260 --> 00:41:11,636
Why is he talking about marbling?

522
00:41:12,971 --> 00:41:14,181
It can't be.

523
00:41:14,264 --> 00:41:15,432
The thing is,

524
00:41:15,515 --> 00:41:17,851
to increase the marbling score,

525
00:41:17,934 --> 00:41:20,228
the beef cattle must be fattened.

526
00:41:20,312 --> 00:41:23,356
And to do that,
instead of letting them graze freely,

527
00:41:23,940 --> 00:41:27,694
they're fed a grain-based diet
such as corn.

528
00:41:28,278 --> 00:41:31,656
To promote fat accumulation
over muscle development,

529
00:41:31,740 --> 00:41:34,868
farmers keep the cattle in small pens
where they can't stay active.

530
00:41:35,368 --> 00:41:37,329
What do you think happens

531
00:41:37,913 --> 00:41:39,497
when cattle are kept in such conditions?

532
00:41:40,123 --> 00:41:43,335
Naturally, they become disease-prone.

533
00:41:43,919 --> 00:41:45,837
What must be done then?

534
00:41:46,588 --> 00:41:48,798
They'd have to be injected
with various medications,

535
00:41:48,882 --> 00:41:50,258
including antibiotics.

536
00:41:50,842 --> 00:41:55,889
Such meat doesn't contain
omega-3 fatty acids that are good for us.

537
00:41:55,972 --> 00:41:59,726
Instead, it contains omega-6 fatty acids
that are harmful to our health.

538
00:42:21,498 --> 00:42:22,332
Umami.

539
00:42:23,208 --> 00:42:26,378
The savory taste from the fat.

540
00:42:27,712 --> 00:42:31,299
The allure of umami on the tongue

541
00:42:32,342 --> 00:42:34,427
makes it impossible to give up fat.

542
00:42:36,471 --> 00:42:39,224
Only three countries in the world
grade beef according to the marbling.

543
00:42:39,307 --> 00:42:42,727
They are Korea, America, and Japan.

544
00:42:43,395 --> 00:42:46,273
Now, here is today's mission.

545
00:42:54,072 --> 00:42:56,825
MAXIMIZE UMAMI WITH PASTURE-RAISED,
LOW-MARBLED CHIKSO BEEF FROM ULLEUNGDO!

546
00:43:05,125 --> 00:43:07,168
MAXIMIZE UMAMI WITH PASTURE-RAISED,
LOW-MARBLED CHIKSO BEEF FROM ULLEUNGDO!

547
00:43:08,795 --> 00:43:09,879
I'm screwed.

548
00:43:10,463 --> 00:43:14,384
Let me introduce
the guest judges for this round.

549
00:43:14,467 --> 00:43:16,886
YOON MAN-SEOP, MEAT SUPPLIER
OH JEONG-HO, HANWOO OMAKASE OWNER

550
00:43:16,970 --> 00:43:19,472
KIM JU-WAN, BBQ RESTAURANT OWNER
GIUSEPPE LOMBARDO, ITALIAN MASTER BUTCHER

551
00:43:19,556 --> 00:43:21,975
MIN GI-JEONG, INFLUENCER
MEATY LOVE, MEAT-LOVER CLUB

552
00:43:22,809 --> 00:43:24,477
We ran a big-data analysis

553
00:43:24,561 --> 00:43:26,813
that considered hundreds of possibilities.

554
00:43:26,896 --> 00:43:28,732
The connection between "grass"

555
00:43:28,815 --> 00:43:31,401
and pasture-raised cattle caught my eye.

556
00:43:32,152 --> 00:43:34,404
I had no idea
that the main ingredient would be

557
00:43:34,487 --> 00:43:35,905
Ulleungdo chikso with almost no fat,

558
00:43:35,989 --> 00:43:39,826
but I did have an inkling
that the mission would be beef-related.

559
00:43:45,623 --> 00:43:46,708
The problem is

560
00:43:47,709 --> 00:43:48,793
umami.

561
00:43:48,877 --> 00:43:51,629
How should we maximize umami?

562
00:43:52,630 --> 00:43:53,631
That's the key.

563
00:43:54,507 --> 00:43:56,926
The meat itself has a great texture,

564
00:43:57,010 --> 00:43:58,678
but it's a fact that it's not savory.

565
00:43:58,762 --> 00:44:02,015
We need something to create the umami,

566
00:44:02,098 --> 00:44:03,683
and I chose a rub over a sauce.

567
00:44:03,767 --> 00:44:07,812
I'll make a complex rub
with various herbs and spices,

568
00:44:08,396 --> 00:44:09,731
and I'll put that onto the meat.

569
00:44:10,398 --> 00:44:12,817
I'll also prepare
a Southern-style barbecue sauce

570
00:44:12,901 --> 00:44:14,194
as a dip on the side.

571
00:44:14,277 --> 00:44:17,572
Of course, there will be a personal twist.

572
00:44:17,655 --> 00:44:20,116
Because this is K-food, after all.

573
00:44:22,202 --> 00:44:24,746
AS THE WINNER OF ROUND 1,
HOW DO YOU FEEL ABOUT ROUND 2?

574
00:44:24,829 --> 00:44:28,333
I worked closely with my team
to prepare for the second round.

575
00:44:29,459 --> 00:44:32,128
I never imagined
the mission would be about marbling.

576
00:44:33,755 --> 00:44:36,591
But a fun idea suddenly came to mind.

577
00:44:37,342 --> 00:44:40,720
A very exciting and interesting idea.

578
00:44:40,804 --> 00:44:43,473
WE HEARD YOU PREPARED TO COOK PUFFERFISH…

579
00:44:43,556 --> 00:44:45,975
YOO IN-SEOP
HEAD CHEF OF SEOP

580
00:45:00,740 --> 00:45:01,741
Perilla leaves.

581
00:45:02,534 --> 00:45:03,451
Cheongyang chillies.

582
00:45:04,494 --> 00:45:05,537
Parsnips.

583
00:45:06,538 --> 00:45:07,956
And super sweet corn.

584
00:45:08,790 --> 00:45:10,458
I don't know what you're making,

585
00:45:12,752 --> 00:45:14,462
but it sure is an odd combination.

586
00:45:20,593 --> 00:45:21,678
How did you know each other?

587
00:45:24,013 --> 00:45:25,432
You and Kim Tae-seop.

588
00:45:28,810 --> 00:45:29,644
Chris K.

589
00:45:32,564 --> 00:45:33,731
Eight years ago…

590
00:45:36,025 --> 00:45:36,860
in Paris.

591
00:45:40,113 --> 00:45:40,947
You were at the cathedral.

592
00:45:45,869 --> 00:45:47,579
What were you doing…

593
00:45:51,833 --> 00:45:53,293
sitting before the dead Kim Tae-seop?

594
00:45:55,587 --> 00:45:56,588
Huh?

595
00:45:57,338 --> 00:45:58,298
Over there.

596
00:46:01,968 --> 00:46:03,470
It looks pretty serious.

597
00:46:03,553 --> 00:46:05,763
What are they talking about
instead of going back to cook?

598
00:46:05,847 --> 00:46:06,723
I don't know.

599
00:46:06,806 --> 00:46:09,851
Our mics won't pick up much
over the fridge motors.

600
00:46:09,934 --> 00:46:11,561
Why are they over there of all places?

601
00:46:25,533 --> 00:46:26,367
That's it?

602
00:46:31,873 --> 00:46:33,625
I'm sure there's more you want to know.

603
00:47:13,414 --> 00:47:14,666
Wait, Chef Park.

604
00:47:14,749 --> 00:47:15,667
Just a minute.

605
00:47:21,923 --> 00:47:23,967
It needs to be drier.
Squeeze out more moisture.

606
00:47:24,050 --> 00:47:24,884
Yes, Chef.

607
00:47:24,968 --> 00:47:27,011
And watch the consistency
as you add the brown rice oil.

608
00:47:27,095 --> 00:47:28,471
Make it as thick as possible, okay?

609
00:47:28,555 --> 00:47:29,389
Yes, Chef.

610
00:47:34,185 --> 00:47:35,562
-Sous-chef.
-Yes?

611
00:47:35,645 --> 00:47:37,814
Put the rice in water
and get the jujube tree branches

612
00:47:37,897 --> 00:47:39,482
-and straw to smoke the meat.
-Yes, Chef.

613
00:47:40,567 --> 00:47:43,319
The soaked rice needs to be simmered
over low heat for at least two hours.

614
00:47:43,403 --> 00:47:44,404
Yes, Chef!

615
00:47:46,406 --> 00:47:50,326
Song, add just enough ginger enzyme
to get rid of the smell.

616
00:47:50,410 --> 00:47:51,244
Yes, Chef.

617
00:48:00,753 --> 00:48:01,588
Let me taste it.

618
00:48:10,597 --> 00:48:11,514
Wow.

619
00:48:15,101 --> 00:48:16,144
Yes!

620
00:48:17,270 --> 00:48:18,396
-Keep stirring it.
-Okay.

621
00:50:02,583 --> 00:50:04,544
I told you to make them thinner!

622
00:50:06,379 --> 00:50:10,383
You need to make it thin enough
so you can see what's inside.

623
00:50:10,466 --> 00:50:12,468
-That's how thin the wheat crepes must be.
-Yes, Chef.

624
00:50:14,178 --> 00:50:15,513
The whole point of this dish

625
00:50:15,596 --> 00:50:17,765
is to let you see what's inside
at a glance.

626
00:50:17,849 --> 00:50:18,891
Yes, Chef.

627
00:50:18,975 --> 00:50:21,185
Did you put these in an ice bath
right after blanching them?

628
00:50:21,269 --> 00:50:23,855
-Well, the ice--
-The texture's gone.

629
00:50:23,938 --> 00:50:25,314
Didn't I tell you
to put it in an ice bath?

630
00:50:25,398 --> 00:50:26,899
I'm sorry, Chef. We'll cook another batch.

631
00:50:29,026 --> 00:50:30,445
Focus, everyone!

632
00:50:32,530 --> 00:50:35,408
Just one mistake
will bring down the entire dish.

633
00:50:35,491 --> 00:50:37,452
-Yes, Chef!
-Yes, Chef.

634
00:50:41,205 --> 00:50:42,373
Michael, how's the sabayon coming?

635
00:50:42,457 --> 00:50:44,208
It needs about ten more minutes.

636
00:50:44,292 --> 00:50:46,043
-I'll add the gochujang then.
-Okay.

637
00:50:46,127 --> 00:50:47,837
If you think it needs more sweetness,
add rice syrup.

638
00:50:47,920 --> 00:50:48,796
Yes, Chef.

639
00:50:48,880 --> 00:50:51,299
-Yeong-su, get a few yams from the pantry.
-Yes, Chef!

640
00:51:34,217 --> 00:51:35,843
I'm fine for now, so you can leave.

641
00:51:35,927 --> 00:51:37,512
I'll call if I need anything.

642
00:52:13,130 --> 00:52:14,799
I told you to leave.

643
00:52:39,615 --> 00:52:41,868
You have ten seconds left.

644
00:52:41,951 --> 00:52:43,077
Ten.

645
00:52:43,661 --> 00:52:44,495
Nine.

646
00:52:44,996 --> 00:52:46,080
Eight.

647
00:52:46,747 --> 00:52:47,582
Seven.

648
00:52:48,457 --> 00:52:49,333
Six.

649
00:52:50,001 --> 00:52:50,835
Five.

650
00:52:51,460 --> 00:52:52,753
Four.

651
00:52:52,837 --> 00:52:53,754
Three.

652
00:52:54,338 --> 00:52:55,464
Two.

653
00:52:55,548 --> 00:52:56,382
One.

654
00:52:56,465 --> 00:52:58,050
The time is up.

655
00:53:00,219 --> 00:53:03,014
HUSH: SOUTHERN US BBQ STEAK
AND A POTATO PLAYGROUND

656
00:53:03,097 --> 00:53:05,349
I used an old Southern US recipe

657
00:53:05,433 --> 00:53:07,476
and slow-grilled the meat
on a charcoal fire.

658
00:53:08,060 --> 00:53:11,188
I focused on increasing the umami
by coating the meat with a rub

659
00:53:11,272 --> 00:53:14,108
made with various Korean herbs and spices.

660
00:53:14,191 --> 00:53:16,652
The best side dish for steak

661
00:53:17,945 --> 00:53:18,821
is potatoes.

662
00:53:19,405 --> 00:53:22,366
I fried and confited potatoes
of various shapes and sizes

663
00:53:23,200 --> 00:53:25,453
and named it "potato playground."

664
00:53:26,120 --> 00:53:28,080
The reason I called it a playground

665
00:53:28,789 --> 00:53:30,625
comes from a personal memory.

666
00:53:31,208 --> 00:53:32,501
This side dish was inspired

667
00:53:33,044 --> 00:53:37,298
by my childhood memories
of eating cotton candy at the playground…

668
00:53:41,344 --> 00:53:42,595
while waiting for my mother.

669
00:53:43,971 --> 00:53:46,849
ONGOJISIM
BEEF NOODLES WITH CORN JANG

670
00:53:47,433 --> 00:53:50,811
If the meat itself lacks fat and flavor,

671
00:53:50,895 --> 00:53:55,733
I thought it'd be better to use it
to create a clean beef broth

672
00:53:55,816 --> 00:53:56,734
than to grill or braise it.

673
00:53:57,526 --> 00:54:00,696
I found some good-quality
super sweet corn in the pantry,

674
00:54:00,780 --> 00:54:03,574
so I made a corn jang
using traditional Korean soy sauce

675
00:54:03,658 --> 00:54:07,536
and used that to season the broth
to further bring out the umami.

676
00:54:08,120 --> 00:54:09,956
I didn't resort to any tricks.

677
00:54:11,666 --> 00:54:13,709
I simply offered a new interpretation

678
00:54:14,543 --> 00:54:16,087
of traditional beef noodles.

679
00:54:17,463 --> 00:54:20,341
EXCEPTION: WHEAT CREPE CARPACCIO
AND GOCHUJANG SABAYON

680
00:54:20,424 --> 00:54:23,386
We didn't have a whole lot of time,

681
00:54:23,469 --> 00:54:25,513
so I used a unique cooking method

682
00:54:25,596 --> 00:54:28,808
to mature and freeze the beef
in a short period of time.

683
00:54:28,891 --> 00:54:33,896
Making a carpaccio requires the meat
to be sliced paper-thin.

684
00:54:47,243 --> 00:54:48,619
This gochujang

685
00:54:48,703 --> 00:54:51,789
will bring out the umami the beef lacks.

686
00:54:52,498 --> 00:54:54,041
The ingredients in the gochujang are…

687
00:54:57,670 --> 00:54:59,005
I'm sorry, but that's a secret.

688
00:55:01,424 --> 00:55:04,301
FAMIGLIA: GRILLED SOUTH AMERICAN ASADO,
PERILLA LEAF CHIMICHURRI, GRILLED CORN

689
00:55:04,927 --> 00:55:06,679
This is grilled
South American-style asado.

690
00:55:07,179 --> 00:55:08,806
I coated the beef with nuruk

691
00:55:08,889 --> 00:55:10,933
and aged it for an hour
before grilling it.

692
00:55:12,309 --> 00:55:15,855
The sauce is a Korean take on chimichurri.

693
00:55:43,883 --> 00:55:45,593
When people think of nuruk,

694
00:55:45,676 --> 00:55:47,762
they think it's only used to make alcohol.

695
00:55:48,345 --> 00:55:49,472
But did you know

696
00:55:49,555 --> 00:55:51,682
that if you spread nuruk on meat
before grilling it,

697
00:55:51,766 --> 00:55:53,601
the umami increases drastically?

698
00:55:54,185 --> 00:55:55,644
It also tenderizes the meat.

699
00:55:57,563 --> 00:55:59,815
Nuruk can be used
to prepare meat for the elderly

700
00:55:59,899 --> 00:56:01,817
who can't chew as well.

701
00:56:10,242 --> 00:56:12,286
Aging meat with nuruk?

702
00:56:15,331 --> 00:56:16,457
This guy is something else.

703
00:56:17,249 --> 00:56:18,542
WHAT IS THE JANGDOKDAE NEXT TO YOU FOR?

704
00:56:18,626 --> 00:56:19,502
This earthenware jar?

705
00:56:21,337 --> 00:56:24,090
It's a natural oven made by hand.

706
00:56:24,715 --> 00:56:26,342
It's basically a nuruk oven.

707
00:56:26,425 --> 00:56:28,469
The inside is coated
with a mixture of sesame oil

708
00:56:28,552 --> 00:56:30,012
and nuruk salt that was layered over time.

709
00:56:30,763 --> 00:56:33,182
If you cook nuruk-treated meat
on any other grill,

710
00:56:33,265 --> 00:56:34,433
the nuruk burns off right away.

711
00:56:35,017 --> 00:56:36,185
So you slowly cook the meat in here,

712
00:56:36,268 --> 00:56:39,063
and it gradually absorbs
the aroma of the nuruk.

713
00:56:39,855 --> 00:56:40,689
The fire?

714
00:56:40,773 --> 00:56:43,692
I burned a mix of straw
and jujube tree branches.

715
00:56:45,194 --> 00:56:46,946
The heat and smoke cook the meat.

716
00:56:47,029 --> 00:56:48,989
ROUND 2 HIGHLIGHTS
JUDGING IN PROGRESS

717
00:56:49,782 --> 00:56:52,159
I like how confident everyone is.

718
00:56:52,243 --> 00:56:53,119
It looks good.

719
00:56:54,537 --> 00:56:55,538
Yeah.

720
00:56:55,621 --> 00:56:58,833
I doubt Chef Cha
will get eliminated in this round.

721
00:56:58,916 --> 00:56:59,917
Don't you agree?

722
00:57:01,919 --> 00:57:02,795
Yeah.

723
00:57:05,131 --> 00:57:07,466
But if

724
00:57:08,467 --> 00:57:11,679
